namespace ScatoloneDownloader.Mtg
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Informal effect ontology applied to cube cards. A card can carry several
    /// effects at once (e.g. an ETB creature that also removes a permanent), so
    /// this is a <see cref="FlagsAttribute"/> bitset combined with <c>|</c>.
    ///
    /// Structural buckets Creature and Land are intentionally NOT effects — they
    /// are covered by <see cref="MacroType"/> and the view tree. This enum holds
    /// only functional effects. Members are authored via the tagging tool and
    /// persisted by name (not packed int) for readable git diffs.
    ///
    /// Deliberately absent, decided 2026-09-05 — every tag is a question asked of
    /// 30k cards, so one that never changes a design decision costs and returns
    /// nothing:
    ///   - FOGS. A Fog prevents a combat, it does not answer a threat. It buys a
    ///     turn rather than saving a permanent, which is why it is not folded into
    ///     <see cref="Protection"/> either, despite also being instant-speed
    ///     prevention.
    ///   - LIFEGAIN. A resource, not an interaction. Worth a tag only for a cube
    ///     with an archetype built on it; this one has none.
    /// </summary>
    [Flags]
    public enum CardEffect
    {
        None            = 0,
        Tokens          = 1 << 0,   // put CREATURE bodies onto the battlefield (Lingering Souls, Bitterblossom, Grave Titan) — and yours, not somebody else's. A Treasure, Clue, Food or Lander token is a resource the card hands over in passing, not a board presence, and Afterlife's Spirit goes to the creature's own controller; neither is what a go-wide deck is built on. Narrowed 2026-09-15, and WIDENED the same day in the other direction: a body need not arrive as a token at all, so earthbend and "all lands are 1/1 creatures" (Nature's Revolt) count — a go-wide deck cares about the creatures, not the wording that made them.
        Removal         = 1 << 1,   // answer ONE creature or planeswalker. A SHRINK counts, ruled 2026-09-15: a creature at zero toughness dies exactly as one that is destroyed, in both vocabularies — "target creature gets -3/-3" and "put a -1/-1 counter on target creature". Two edges matter. The toughness has to actually go down, so Icy-style "-2/-0" is Pacify, not this; and it has to be aimed at ONE named creature, because "each creature gets -1/-1" is a Wipe and is tagged as one. Widened 2026-09-17 so every rule here reads a QUALIFIED target — "damage to target ATTACKING creature", "target creature AN OPPONENT CONTROLS gets -X/-X" — and reads a fireball divided among several things, and an EDICT in any wording provided it is aimed at THEM ("each player sacrifices" costs you one too). Two shapes are deliberately out: a NONCREATURE target, which ends in the same letters and was reading as a kill, and an answer that can only touch what is already blocking the card, which is a combat trick.
        Counter         = 1 << 2,   // answer a spell on the stack by countering it
        RemovePermanent = 1 << 3,   // Vindicate / remove any permanent
        Wipe            = 1 << 4,   // mass removal: the board is emptied, by any verb. Destroy, exile, damage, shrink, or return it all to hand — Desynchronization is a Wrath that happens to give the cards back, and "each player sacrifices all but one" is a Wrath somebody else performs. Widened 2026-09-15 to read past the ADJECTIVE, which is where half the sweepers in the game put one: Anarchy destroys all WHITE permanents, Perish all GREEN creatures, Evaporate damages each WHITE AND/OR BLUE one. Deliberately excluded: "destroy all creatures blocking or blocked by this creature" (Abu Ja'far, Kjeldoran Frostbeast, the Glyph cycle), which reads like a sweeper and is a combat trick.
        Bounce          = 1 << 5,   // unsummon / boomerang — a TARGETED return to hand (or a mass one). "Return this creature to its owner's hand" as an activation cost or an end-step drawback is a price the card pays, not an answer it offers. Narrowed 2026-09-15.
        Ramp            = 1 << 6,   // more mana, or sooner: a mana ability on a creature or rock, a land put onto the battlefield. A land tapping for its own single mana is just a land — with ONE exception, ruled 2026-09-15: a land that makes more mana than it costs EVERY turn (Ancient Tomb, Mishra's Workshop, the Karoo lands once they have given a land back). Mana that arrives without a {T} on a permanent you keep is also Ramp, ruled 2026-09-15: Black Lotus, Lotus Petal and Blood Pet spend THEMSELVES, and Dark Ritual is the same trade written as a spell. The one exception is a LAND THAT ENTERS TAPPED — Crystal Vein cracks for two and counts, Dwarven Ruins and Ebon Stronghold do the same thing and do not, because entering tapped costs the turn the extra mana was meant to buy. Eight more families ruled 2026-09-18, all of them mana you never had to make or a land you never had to draw: COST REDUCTION for other spells (the Medallions, the Crystals — but not "this spell costs {1} less", which was tagged 0 of 40); ANY activated mana ability on a non-land, whatever it charges (a tap for Llanowar Elves, a counter for Wall of Roots, a card out of hand for Elvish Spirit Guide, charge counters for the Mana Batteries); EXTRA LAND DROPS; MANA MULTIPLIERS (Mana Flare, High Tide, Wild Growth); a LAND OUT OF YOUR HAND onto the battlefield; UNTAPPING LANDS; a LAND ONTO THE BATTLEFIELD by any wording; and TREASURES, but only several at once or over and over — one Treasure once is a rider, the line already drawn for the Clue. Against all of that stands one subtraction, and it is the same ruling read backwards: MANA THAT COSTS MANA adds none, it converts colour, so Celestial Prism and Fire Sprites are ManaFixing and not Ramp — unless the trade comes back up, as Astrolabe pays {1} for two. Asked of every mana line on the card, so one free ability beside a paid one keeps the tag (Mana Prism). Two things the land family does NOT include, both learned by breaking them: the land handed to the player a removal spell was aimed at is their consolation, not your ramp (Emergency Eject, Price of Freedom), and ONE land sacrificed for ONE land is a swap (Renewal) while one for TWO is not (Harrow). The LANDER token is not in either exclusion and fetches a real land — all 17 reviewed cards that make one are Ramp.
        Disenchant      = 1 << 7,
        Discard         = 1 << 8,   // strip cards from somebody else's HAND (Mind Rot, Hymn to Tourach, Liliana of the Veil). Like Mill, only outward: a bare "discard a card" is nearly always a cost — madness, blitz, cycling reminder text, the back half of looting — and paying it is not attacking anyone. Narrowed 2026-09-15.
        CardAdvantage   = 1 << 9,   // a card the opponent does not get: draw two or more, a draw you can go back to (an activated ability or a recurring trigger — an ETB fires once and is a cantrip), or "draw a card for each X", which is the same thing counted the other way round. PARITY is not advantage, and it wears three disguises: a loot draws and discards in one breath (that is Filter alone, ruled 2026-09-04), cycling pays a card to replace itself, and an ability that sacrifices its own permanent runs once. The CLUE and the IMPULSE are the borderline cases and both answer the same question: one card, once? A single Clue has to be cashed for {2} and a single impulse replaces the card that made it, so each is a rider — they earn the tag when the card makes them REPEATEDLY, and an impulse earns it outright when it takes more than one card off the top, which is a draw two wearing a different coat. Ruled 2026-09-11, parity carved out 2026-09-15, the one-for-one line drawn 2026-09-15. Parity is COUNTED, from 2026-09-16: a loot that draws three and discards one is a Careful Study with a bonus, so only a provable gain rescues the tag and anything unreadable stays parity. The same day closed four more ways the card is handed back without the word "discard" — Dream Cache puts it on top, Lat-Nam's Legacy shuffles it in, Jandor's Ring pays back the card just drawn. Three more shapes ruled 2026-09-16: the TOP OF YOUR LIBRARY is a second hand (Fblthp, Glarb, the Traveling Chocobo never run out even though they never draw); LOOK AT N AND TAKE MORE THAN ONE is a draw with selection, while taking exactly one is Filter; and CASTING OUT OF A GRAVEYARD OR EXILE counts only when you can go back to it every turn (Festival of Embers, Edgar), never for one flashback card.
        Filter          = 1 << 10,
        Reanimate       = 1 << 11,
        Buff            = 1 << 12,   // raise POWER and/or TOUGHNESS — that is the whole test, ruled 2026-09-18. A granted KEYWORD is not Buff, however good it is: flying, deathtouch, lifelink, first strike, indestructible change what a creature does, not what its numbers are, and the hand-tagging says so 49 cards to 5 (Jump and Cloak of Feathers are the same sentence and only one was tagged). DOUBLE STRIKE is the single exception, because doubling the damage is doubling the power by another name. A pump sized by X or by a count is a pump too. Only when it lands on SOMETHING ELSE (Giant Growth, Glorious Anthem, Bonesplitter): a creature that pumps only itself has a stat line, not an effect, so "{2}{R}: This creature gets +3/+0" and "Uril gets +2/+2 for each Aura attached to it" stay untagged. A +1/+1 COUNTER on somebody else is the same effect in the other vocabulary and counts too, ruled 2026-09-15 — one counter on one creature, support, distribute, all of it; only counters aimed at an OPPONENT'S creatures stay out. That ruling went AGAINST the measurement: of the 161 reviewed cards putting a counter on somebody else only 46 were tagged, the two halves were indistinguishable by wording, and 113 reviewed entries were realigned by hand the same day. It is the largest single correction the ontology has taken. The other half of that ruling points the other way: a pump restricted to one creature TYPE is NOT Buff — "Minotaur creatures get +1/+0" promises a payoff a cube with no Minotaur deck cannot collect. A COLOUR is not a tribe (Crusade, Bad Moon keep it) and neither is a STATE (Castle's "untapped creatures you control"); a lord that also pumps something unrestricted keeps it too.
        Protection      = 1 << 13,   // keep something alive, for somebody other than the card itself. TWO vocabularies, ruled equal on 2026-09-15: the keyword shield GRANTED to something else (Mother of Runes, Mystic Veil), and DAMAGE PREVENTION aimed at something else (Circle of Protection, Healing Salve, Orim). They do the same job — you hold them up and something survives — and the hand-tagged cards split 47 to 44 between them. Both clear the same gate: deployable in RESPONSE (an instant, flash, or an activated ability), so a bogle born with hexproof and a static Bubble Matrix are out; "This spell can't be countered" is the same self-shield written for the stack. Three shapes of prevention are deliberately excluded: a shield the card puts on ITSELF, a fog (it names no recipient), and preventing what a creature DEALS, which neutralises it and is Pacify — Maze of Ith and Gaseous Form are tagged that way. Regeneration is NOT in: measured, "regenerate target" cost 12 wrong for 4 right, so Death Ward stays untagged. Decided 2026-09-05, widened 2026-09-15.
        Burn            = 1 << 14,   // direct damage at a FACE (Lightning Bolt, Fireblast, Price of Progress). Damage pointed at a creature is how the game kills creatures and is already Removal, so Explosive Shot is Removal alone; "any target" is both, because it can go either way. A sweeper is Burn only when it catches the players too — Inferno's "each creature and each player" is Wipe AND Burn, Pyroclasm is Wipe alone. Damage to YOURSELF (Ancient Tomb, City of Brass, Juzam Djinn) is a price the card charges, not an effect. Ruled 2026-09-15.
        Sacrifice       = 1 << 15,   // a sacrifice OUTLET for your own creatures (Ashnod's Altar, Goblin Bombardment, Viscera Seer) — the half of the combo that makes a stolen creature worth taking, so read it next to Steal. NOT an edict, which empties somebody else's board (that is Removal's business), and not "as an additional cost to cast this spell, sacrifice a creature", which is a price paid once rather than a place to put things. Creatures, artifacts and anything named as a permanent all count — Atog and Infernal Tribute are Ashnod's Altar with different fuel — but LANDS do not: "sacrifice a land" is Harrow paying for a fetch, never an engine. Ruled 2026-09-15.
        Steal           = 1 << 16,   // threaten / act of treason / control magic (temporary or permanent control theft)
        Tutor           = 1 << 17,   // search library for a card (Demonic Tutor, Green Sun's Zenith, creature fetch) — selection/consistency, not raw card advantage
        ManaFixing      = 1 << 18,   // mana colour fixing (dual/fetch lands, multicolour rocks) — distinct from Ramp; a card can be both. Two shapes added 2026-09-15 that never say "or" on one line: TYPECYCLING, which fetches the colour you are short of, and a land with TWO mana abilities in different colours (Bleachbone Verge and the whole modern cycle). One shape taken out: mana you may ONLY SPEND on one thing is not fixing, it buys the card the designer had in mind — judged per line, so a card with one restricted ability and one free one still counts. A SEARCH FOR A LAND was measured and rejected once, then split by DESTINATION on 2026-09-18 and half of it came back: fetched to HAND it fixes the colour you are short of and ramps nothing, because the land still has to be played off your one land drop (tagged on 28 of 30 reviewed cards); put straight onto the BATTLEFIELD it is the extra mana rather than the colour, and stays Ramp (8 of 51). Asked undivided the family is 42% and reads like noise, which is what the first measurement saw. The other ruling of that day cuts the opposite way: mana you get for NOTHING BUT A TAP is a mana SOURCE, so the card is Ramp and the colour is a property of the source rather than a service — Birds of Paradise, Mox Jasper, Solar Array are Ramp alone (a bare {T} for any colour was tagged on 2 of 28). Pay something on top — mana, a life, the tap of another permanent — and it converts colour instead of making it, which IS the job: Celestial Prism, Mana Prism, Gene Pollinator (11 of 12). A LAND is exempt from that and never asked, because a land makes no mana you did not already have and the colour is the only thing it gives you (17 of 17). Two more added the same day: EVERY LAND FLAVOUR OF CYCLING, where "basic landcycling" alone is printed on 125 cards — more than all five named types together — while slivercycling and wizardcycling fetch a body and stay out; and the TREASURE, which is a Lotus Petal in token form and fixes however many you get, the count deciding only whether it also ramps. Named in full: bare "Fixing" read as "fixing what?" (mana or hand), and the tagger shows this name verbatim. Old "Fixing" tags still parse via the alias table.
        Pacify          = 1 << 19,   // soft/pseudo removal: neutralise SOMEBODY ELSE'S creature without destroying it (tap-lock/Icy Manipulator, Pacifism/Arrest, detain), INCLUDING by preventing what it deals — Maze of Ith and Gaseous Form, the reading ruled on 2026-09-05 and finally implemented on 2026-09-15. Three things are not this tag, all of them the same question asked again: UNTAPPING a creature (the rules were reading "untap target creature" as "tap target creature", which was a quarter of the false positives), tapping a creature YOU control as a cost (Energy Tap), and a card that stops YOUR OWN creatures attacking (Akron Legionnaire). The prevention half asks the same question twice more: Mtenda Lion prevents what IT deals, which blunts its own attack, and Ebony Horse prevents what a creature YOU CONTROL deals, which is vigilance bought at instant speed — Foxfire says almost the same sentence and is Pacify, so the whole difference is "you control". And a lock the OPPONENT CAN BUY OUT OF is a tax rather than a lock — Heroism and Winter's Chill let the attacker pay and come through, so whether anything is neutralised is their decision (ruled 2026-09-16, "non c'è certezza"). Unconditional prevention IS the tag: Safeguard, Warning and Subdue say what Horn of Deafening says and were realigned to match. A card that only restrains ITSELF is a stat line, not an effect: "this creature can't attack" is defender, printed on a Wall, and "can't attack unless defending player controls an Island" is a drawback. Narrowed 2026-09-15, which was the single largest source of noise on the board.
        LandDestruction = 1 << 20,   // destroy/exile lands (Stone Rain, Sinkhole, Wasteland, Armageddon). Deliberately narrow: attacking the mana base by removing lands, NOT the wider "mana denial" family (Winter Orb, Blood Moon, Sphere effects), which stays untagged. Distinct from RemovePermanent, whose rules only read the literal "destroy target permanent" of a Vindicate.
        Mill            = 1 << 21,   // put cards from a library into a graveyard (Glimpse the Unthinkable, Millstone, Altar of Dementia). Only when it is aimed at SOMEBODY ELSE. A bare "mill three cards" fills your own graveyard, which is fuel for what the card does next rather than an attack on anybody — Stinkweed Imp's dredge and Fell Gravship's self-mill are graveyard cards, not mill cards. Narrowed 2026-09-15; the older cost exclusion (Deep Spawn, Millikin) follows from the same reading, since a price is always paid by yourself.
        Regrowth        = 1 << 22,   // return a card from a graveyard to HAND (Regrowth, Raise Dead, Eternal Witness). Deliberately NOT merged with Reanimate, which puts it straight onto the battlefield: one hands back a card you still have to pay for, the other cheats the cost — different speeds, different decks.
        Redirect        = 1 << 23,   // act on a spell already on the stack WITHOUT countering it: change its target (Misdirection, Deflection) or copy it (Fork, Reverberate, Twincast). Kept as one tag because both do the same job in a deck — they turn an opponent's spell into your problem-solver — and because splitting them would give two tags of a few dozen cards each. Distinct from Counter, which answers by removing the spell.
    }
}
